---
// Life on networks: from one long row to a grid to contacts anywhere in the room, and the room becomes a network of
// nodes (F1); the Life-like network automata as two rings round a node, at ever finer resolution, the first contribution
// of the thesis (F2); and the three Life-like rules of slide C4 again, now on three kinds of network (F3). All come
// before the thesis map, so none has the ribbon. Formulas are LaTeX (./tex).
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import Seats from './Seats.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { tex, texify, phi } from './tex';

// F2: the two rings in design pixels of the slide (src/scripts/defence/donut.ts draws them)
const rings = [{ x: 500, y: 540, R: 215, w: 92, on: false }, { x: 1420, y: 540, R: 215, w: 92, on: true }];
// F3: Maze, Life and Replicator, as Life-like network automata at resolution 9, and the three kinds of network
const likes = [
  { id: 'maze', name: 'Maze', B: 8, S: 62 },
  { id: 'life', name: 'Game of Life', B: 8, S: 12 },
  { id: 'replicator', name: 'Replicator', B: 170, S: 170 },
];
const nets = ['small-world ring', 'small-world grid', 'scale-free'];
---
<Slide label="Generalising even further" builds={5} class="f1">
  <div class="row grow f1-row">
    <div class="f1-room">
      <canvas class="f1-links" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <Seats you={[3, 4]} label="The room: everyone in one long row, then everyone linked to the eight people around them, then links rewired across the room" />
    </div>
    <div class="col f1-side">
      <h2>Generalising even further</h2>
      <ol class="f1-stages">
        <li data-b="1">Elementary cellular automata</li>
        <li data-b="2">Life-like cellular automata</li>
        <li data-b="3">Life-like <b>network</b> automata<label class="f1-p">rewired<input type="range" class="ctl" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.2" data-p aria-label="share of links rewired" /><output data-pv>20%</output></label></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">What if the room played the game differently? In the game you looked at your two neighbours, so the room was really one long row: link everybody to the person before and after them, snaking up from the back of the room to the front and round again, and everyone has two neighbours. That is a one-dimensional cellular automaton, an elementary one, like the rows of cells we just saw.</p>
  <p slot="say">Now link everyone to the eight people around them: a grid, like the one the Game of Life and the other Life-like cellular automata live on. The room wraps round at its edges, so the people at a wall have eight too; the stubs at the edges are links to the far side.</p>
  <p slot="say">Then take some of those links and swing them across the room to anyone at all. A few long links like these make a small-world network: everyone is suddenly only a few steps from everyone else, and running the same Life-like rule for everyone on it gives a Life-like network automaton. On this page, drag the slider: at 0 the grid comes back, at 100% the links are completely random.</p>
  <p slot="say">But now some have four contacts and some thirteen, so a rule like “exactly three neighbours” no longer makes sense for everybody. From here on the people become nodes, and a hand up becomes a node that is on.</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Life-like network automata" builds={4} class="f2">
  <h2>Life-like network automata <span class="count">(∞)</span></h2>
  <canvas class="f2-rings" data-rings={JSON.stringify(rings)} aria-label="The rule as two rings of intervals of the share of neighbours that are on: where a node that is off is born, and where a node that is on survives"></canvas>
  {rings.map((g) => <p class="f2-lab" style={`left: calc(${g.x} * var(--u)); top: calc(${g.y - g.R - 105} * var(--u))`}>{g.on ? 'Surviving' : 'Being born'}</p>)}
  <div class="f2-mid">
    <p class="f2-phi" set:html={phi(5, 6, 11, true)} />
    <label class="f2-res"><span set:html={tex('r')} /><input type="range" class="ctl" min="2" max="13" step="1" value="5" data-r aria-label="resolution" /></label>
    <button type="button" class="ctl f2-cousin" data-cousin>⇄ its cousin</button>
  </div>
  <p class="result f2-result" data-b="4"><b>Our first contribution</b> Defining LLNAs to allow symmetry</p>
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('A node with ten neighbours and a node with three cannot follow a rule that counts neighbours. So count the share instead: the fraction of a node’s neighbours that are on, which runs round each ring from 0 at the top, clockwise, to 1, from light to dark. Cut it into intervals, five here. A node that is off (left) is born when the share falls in one of its chosen intervals; a node that is on (right) survives when the share falls in one of its chosen intervals. The chosen intervals are the outlined ones. An end drawn solid belongs to the interval, a dotted one does not: in $\\phi^5_{6,11}$ a node that is off is born from $\\tfrac15$ up to and including $\\tfrac35$. On this page, click an interval to choose it or not. The rule’s name reads the chosen intervals as two binary numbers, as rule 90 did on the elementary table: born is 6, the second and third intervals, and survive is 11, the first, second and fourth.')} />
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('Each click cuts the share more finely, into 7, 9 and then 11 intervals: the resolution $r$. The rule keeps the regions it had, but its name changes, and every finer resolution can write rules that a coarser one cannot: there is no end to them. At $r = 9$, on a grid where everyone has eight neighbours, each interval holds exactly one number of live neighbours, and the Game of Life is $\\phi^9_{8,12}$. The slider on this page sets any resolution from 2 to 13.')} />
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('Defining these rules properly, for any network and at any resolution, is the first contribution of the thesis (Chapter 5, Physica D 2025), and the point of the definition is symmetry. Which ends each interval includes is chosen so that every rule has a cousin that behaves exactly the same with on and off swapped: the same patterns, in the other colours. The button on this page swaps a rule for its cousin: born becomes the complement of survive read backwards, and survive the complement of born read backwards; some rules are their own cousin. At an odd resolution the intervals below $\\tfrac12$ include their left end, those above it their right end, and the middle one both. An even resolution has no middle interval, so there the two rings give $\\tfrac12$ to different sides: the born ring to the interval just left of it, the survive ring to the one just right of it (Appendix C of the thesis).')} />
</Slide>

<Slide label="Any rule, any network" builds={5} class="f3">
  <div class="f3-head">
    <h2>Any rule, any network</h2>
    <div class="f3-nets" role="group" aria-label="the network">
      {nets.map((n, i) => <button type="button" class="ctl" data-net={i} aria-pressed={i ? 'false' : 'true'}>{n}</button>)}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="f3-likes">
    {likes.map((g) => (
      <figure>
        <div class="f3-box">
          <canvas data-rule={`${g.B},${g.S}`} aria-label={`${g.name} on a small network, from a random start`}></canvas>
          <SimButton class="f3-btn" />
        </div>
        <figcaption><b>{g.name}</b> <span set:html={phi(9, g.B, g.S)} /></figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The same three rules as before, Maze, the Game of Life and Replicator, now as Life-like network automata at resolution 9, each started with its own button, all three from the same random start, on a ring of 60 nodes, each linked to its four nearest neighbours on either side, with some of the links rewired. Maze still freezes and Replicator still churns, but the Game of Life soon dies out here. Each reset draws a new start.</p>
  <p slot="say">Two more clicks put the same three rules on other networks: a grid of 100 nodes with eight neighbours each, again with some links rewired, and a network of 100 nodes grown by preferential attachment, with a few hubs. Both the rule and the network can be changed, and every combination behaves in its own way: a great many situations to study.</p>
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('A word on the names. The ring and the grid are Watts–Strogatz networks (WSr and WSg in the thesis): a ring or a grid with some of its links rewired at random. “Small-world” is a shorthand: depending on how much is rewired, such a network is close to the lattice, a small world, or close to a random network, and most of the rewired networks in the thesis lean towards random. The third is grown by nonlinear preferential attachment (NPA): every newcomer links to earlier nodes with a probability that grows with their degree $k$, as $k^\\alpha + 1$. With $\\alpha = 1$, as here, it resembles a Barabási–Albert scale-free network without being quite one; other values of $\\alpha$ give no hubs at all ($\\alpha = 0$) or one giant hub.')} />
</Slide>

<style>
  .f1-row { gap: calc(80 * var(--u)); align-items: stretch; }
  .f1-room { position: relative; flex: none; margin-top: calc(-30 * var(--u)); }
  .f1-room :global(.seats) { position: relative; height: calc(930 * var(--u)); width: auto; }
  .f1-links { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .f1-side { justify-content: center; gap: calc(32 * var(--u)); }
  .f1-stages { font-size: calc(50 * var(--u)); gap: calc(30 * var(--u)); padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .f1-stages li { transition: color 0.35s ease, opacity 0.35s ease; }
  .f1-stages li.past { color: var(--muted); }
  .f1-p { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(20 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(14 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(36 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); }
  .f1-p input { width: calc(560 * var(--u)); }
  .f1-p output { min-width: 3em; }

  :global(.f1.as-nodes) :global(.seat) { opacity: 0; }

  .f2-rings { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .f2-lab {
    position: absolute; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); font-size: calc(54 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; white-space: nowrap;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .f2-mid {
    position: absolute; left: calc(960 * var(--u)); top: calc(540 * var(--u)); transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: calc(48 * var(--u));
  }
  .f2-phi { font-size: calc(78 * var(--u)); white-space: nowrap; }
  .f2-res { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(18 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); }
  .f2-res input { width: calc(300 * var(--u)); }
  .f2-cousin { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); min-width: calc(330 * var(--u)); }
  .f2-cousin[data-own] { color: var(--muted); }
  .f2-result { margin-top: auto; position: relative; }

  .f3-head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-nets { display: flex; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-nets .ctl { font-size: calc(30 * var(--u)); font-weight: 600; }
  .f3-nets .ctl[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); border-color: var(--ink); }
  .f3-likes { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex: 1; align-items: center; }
  .f3-likes figure { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-box { position: relative; }
  .f3-likes canvas { width: calc(540 * var(--u)); height: calc(540 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-box :global(.f3-btn) { right: calc(-22 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(-22 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-likes figcaption { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: calc(6 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); }
  .f3-likes figcaption span { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, fresh, type How } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { picker, setPhi, type Ring } from '../../scripts/defence/donut';
  import { cousin } from '../../scripts/defence/rings';
  import { picture, drawNet, type Picture } from '../../scripts/defence/netdraw';
  import { buildNet, makeRng, type NetSpec, type Layout } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { step, randomState, type Rule } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { room, wire, degrees, COLS, ROWS, SEATS } from '../../scripts/defence/room';

  // ── F1: the room, in three stages. Everyone in one long row, as in the experiment: a ring that snakes up from the back
  // of the room to the front and closes over the back edge (build 1); everyone linked to the eight people around them,
  // the links growing out of each person around the row (build 2); a share p of the links rewired across the room, live
  // on a slider, the moving ends swinging over (build 3). Then the fewest and the most contacts circled (build 4), and
  // the people become nodes (build 5). The room wraps round at its edges, so a link over an edge is drawn as a stub on
  // either side. The links (and the nodes) are drawn on a canvas behind the seats, so a tween repaints the canvas and
  // never the eighty people. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f1')) {
    const svg = s.querySelector<SVGSVGElement>('svg.seats')!, over = svg.querySelector('[data-over]')!, canvas = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.f1-links')!;
    const seats = Array.from(svg.querySelectorAll<SVGUseElement>('use.seat')), stages = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.f1-stages li'));
    const slider = s.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-p]')!, pv = s.querySelector<HTMLOutputElement>('[data-pv]')!;
    // Seed 114895: "you" keeps eight contacts at every p on the slider, as the next slides assume (npm test checks it).
    const you = 4 * COLS + 3, P0 = 0.2, R = room(114895), n = R.links.length, ns = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg', VIEW = 880, GX = 110, GY = 128, ROW_MS = 2200;
    const cx = (i: number) => (i % COLS) * GX + 48, cy = (i: number) => 96 + Math.floor(i / COLS) * GY + 70;
    // where a link's moving end rests when the link is home: its seat, or for a link over an edge, that seat's place
    // beyond the edge
    const hx = (k: number) => cx(R.links[k].stay) + R.links[k].dx * GX, hy = (k: number) => cy(R.links[k].stay) + R.links[k].dy * GY;
    const edge = R.links.map((l, k) => hx(k) !== cx(l.home) || hy(k) !== cy(l.home));
    // the long row: the back row left to right, the next one right to left, and so on to the front, then over the back edge
    const ROW = Array.from({ length: SEATS }, (_, i) => { const q = Math.floor(i / COLS), c = i % COLS; return (ROWS - 1 - q) * COLS + (q % 2 ? COLS - 1 - c : c); });
    const pair = (a: number, b: number) => (a < b ? a * SEATS + b : b * SEATS + a), rowPairs = new Set(ROW.map((a, i) => pair(a, ROW[(i + 1) % SEATS])));
    const inRow = R.links.map((l) => rowPairs.has(pair(l.stay, l.home)));
    // each link's moving end: drawn at (x, y), on its way from (fx, fy) to where it belongs; `moving` lists the links in flight
    const x = new Float32Array(n), y = new Float32Array(n), fx = new Float32Array(n), fy = new Float32Array(n);
    let to = wire(R, 0), at = 0, t0 = 0, dur = 1, raf = 0, moving: number[] = [], shown = '', grown = SEATS, rowRaf = 0;
    const tx = (k: number) => (to[k] === R.links[k].home ? hx(k) : cx(to[k])), ty = (k: number) => (to[k] === R.links[k].home ? hy(k) : cy(to[k]));
    R.links.forEach((_, k) => { x[k] = hx(k); y[k] = hy(k); });
    const mine = (k: number) => R.links[k].stay === you || to[k] === you;
    function draw() {
      const st = stage(canvas); if (!st || at < 1) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, k = st.w / VIEW, paths = [new Path2D(), new Path2D()];
      const seg = (own: boolean, ax: number, ay: number, bx: number, by: number) => { paths[+own].moveTo(ax * k, ay * k); paths[+own].lineTo(bx * k, by * k); };
      if (at === 1) for (let i = 0; i < Math.ceil(grown); i++) {
        // the row so far, the last link partly; the closing link goes over the back edge: a stub at the front and the back
        const a = ROW[i], b = ROW[(i + 1) % SEATS], u = Math.min(1, grown - i), own = a === you || b === you;
        if (i < SEATS - 1) seg(own, cx(a), cy(a), cx(a) + (cx(b) - cx(a)) * u, cy(a) + (cy(b) - cy(a)) * u);
        else { seg(own, cx(a), cy(a), cx(a), cy(a) - (GY / 2) * u); seg(own, cx(b), cy(b), cx(b), cy(b) + (GY / 2) * u); }
      }
      else R.links.forEach((l, j) => {
        const sx = cx(l.stay), sy = cy(l.stay), own = mine(j);
        if (edge[j]) {
          // a link over an edge, while its end is on the way out over it: out at one side, back in at the other
          const gx = hx(j) - sx, gy = hy(j) - sy, ex = x[j] - sx, ey = y[j] - sy, f = (ex * gx + ey * gy) / (gx * gx + gy * gy);
          if (Math.abs(ex * gy - ey * gx) < 1 && f >= 0 && f <= 1.0001) {
            seg(own, sx, sy, sx + gx * Math.min(f, 0.5), sy + gy * Math.min(f, 0.5));
            if (f > 0.5) seg(own, cx(l.home) - gx / 2, cy(l.home) - gy / 2, cx(l.home) - gx * (1 - f), cy(l.home) - gy * (1 - f));
            return;
          }
        }
        seg(own, sx, sy, x[j], y[j]);
      });
      // the row on its own is drawn boldly, since it mostly runs behind the people; in the grid it is one link among many
      const bold = at === 1;
      ctx.lineCap = 'round';
      ctx.strokeStyle = t.muted; ctx.globalAlpha = bold ? 0.9 : 0.45; ctx.lineWidth = (bold ? 4 : 2) * k; ctx.stroke(paths[0]);
      ctx.strokeStyle = t.accent; ctx.globalAlpha = 1; ctx.lineWidth = (bold ? 4 : 2.5) * k; ctx.stroke(paths[1]);
      if (at >= 5) for (let i = 0; i < SEATS; i++) {
        ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(cx(i) * k, cy(i) * k, 15 * k, 0, 2 * Math.PI);
        ctx.fillStyle = i === you ? t.accent : t.panel; ctx.fill();
        ctx.lineWidth = 3 * k; ctx.strokeStyle = i === you ? t.accent : t.ink; ctx.stroke();
      }
    }
    const frame = (now: number) => {
      const u = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / dur), e = u < 0.5 ? 2 * u * u : 1 - (-2 * u + 2) ** 2 / 2;
      for (const j of moving) { x[j] = fx[j] + (tx(j) - fx[j]) * e; y[j] = fy[j] + (ty(j) - fy[j]) * e; }
      draw();
      raf = u < 1 ? requestAnimationFrame(frame) : 0;
    };
    /** Move every link's end to where it belongs at p: over `ms`, or at once; `grow` starts each link (but the row's) at the person who keeps it. */
    function go(p: number, ms: number, grow = false) {
      to = wire(R, p);
      cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; moving = [];
      R.links.forEach((l, j) => {
        if (grow && !inRow[j]) { x[j] = cx(l.stay); y[j] = cy(l.stay); }
        fx[j] = x[j]; fy[j] = y[j];
        if (x[j] !== tx(j) || y[j] !== ty(j)) moving.push(j);
      });
      if (ms > 0 && moving.length) { t0 = performance.now(); dur = ms; raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame); }
      else { for (const j of moving) { x[j] = tx(j); y[j] = ty(j); } moving = []; }
      mark(); draw();
    }
    /** The long row grows from the back of the room to the front. */
    function growRow() {
      const start = performance.now();
      const tick = (now: number) => { grown = SEATS * Math.min(1, (now - start) / ROW_MS); draw(); rowRaf = grown < SEATS ? requestAnimationFrame(tick) : 0; };
      grown = 0; draw(); rowRaf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }
    const stopRow = () => { cancelAnimationFrame(rowRaf); rowRaf = 0; grown = SEATS; };
    // who is highlighted: you, your contacts; from build 4 the fewest and the most contacts, circled, the number up and
    // out of the circle, in the gap between the seats
    function mark() {
      const near = new Set<number>();
      if (at === 1) { near.add(you - 1); near.add(you + 1); }
      if (at >= 2) R.links.forEach((l, j) => { if (l.stay === you) near.add(to[j]); else if (to[j] === you) near.add(l.stay); });
      seats.forEach((u, i) => { u.classList.toggle('you', at >= 1 && i === you); u.classList.toggle('near', near.has(i)); });
      const deg = degrees(R, to), lo = deg.indexOf(Math.min(...deg)), hi = deg.indexOf(Math.max(...deg));
      const want = at >= 4 ? `${lo}:${deg[lo]} ${hi}:${deg[hi]}` : '';
      if (want === shown) return;
      shown = want; over.replaceChildren();
      if (want) for (const i of [lo, hi]) {
        const c = document.createElementNS(ns, 'circle'), tx = document.createElementNS(ns, 'text'), right = cx(i) > 440;
        c.setAttribute('cx', String(cx(i))); c.setAttribute('cy', String(cy(i))); c.setAttribute('r', '62'); c.setAttribute('fill', 'none'); c.setAttribute('stroke', 'var(--danger)'); c.setAttribute('stroke-width', '6');
        tx.setAttribute('x', String(cx(i) + (right ? -52 : 52))); tx.setAttribute('y', String(cy(i) - 52)); tx.setAttribute('text-anchor', right ? 'end' : 'start'); tx.setAttribute('font-size', '46'); tx.setAttribute('font-weight', '700'); tx.setAttribute('fill', 'var(--danger)'); tx.setAttribute('font-family', 'var(--sans)');
        tx.textContent = String(deg[i]); over.append(c, tx);
      }
    }
    const pct = (p: number) => `${Math.round(p * 100)}%`;
    const setP = (p: number) => { slider.value = String(p); pv.value = pct(p); };
    slider.addEventListener('input', () => { const p = +slider.value; pv.value = pct(p); go(p, 450); });
    slide(s, {
      set(b: number, how: How) {
        const was = at; at = b;
        s.classList.toggle('as-nodes', b >= 5);
        stages.forEach((li, i) => li.classList.toggle('past', i + 1 < Math.min(b, 3)));
        stopRow();
        if (b <= 1) { go(0, 0); if (b === 1 && how === 'next') growRow(); return; }
        if (b === 2) { go(0, how === 'next' ? 1100 : was > 2 ? 450 : 0, how === 'next'); return; }
        if (b === 3 && how === 'next' && was === 2) { setP(P0); go(P0, 1400); return; }
        if (was < 2 || how === 'jump') go(+slider.value, 0); else { mark(); draw(); }
      },
      enter() { setP(P0); },
      leave: stopRow,
    });
    setP(P0); mark();
    onSize(canvas, draw);
  }

  // ── F2: the rule as two rings (src/scripts/defence/donut.ts): φ⁵₆,₁₁ at resolution 5, then 7, 9 and 11, one per click
  // (builds 0–3), the chosen regions kept; then our contribution (build 4). The slider sets any resolution from 2 to 13,
  // a click on an interval changes the rule, and the button swaps the rule for its cousin. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f2')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.f2-rings')!, rings = JSON.parse(c.dataset.rings!) as [Ring, Ring];
    const slider = s.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-r]')!, label = s.querySelector('.f2-phi')!, swap = s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-cousin]')!;
    const START = { r: 5, B: 6, S: 11 }, RS = [5, 7, 9, 11];
    const p = picker(c, rings, START, (rule) => {
      setPhi(label, rule); slider.value = String(rule.r);
      const twin = cousin(rule), own = twin.B === rule.B && twin.S === rule.S; swap.toggleAttribute('data-own', own); swap.textContent = own ? 'its own cousin' : '⇄ its cousin';
    });
    slider.addEventListener('input', () => p.setR(+slider.value));
    swap.addEventListener('click', () => p.cousin());
    slide(s, {
      set(b: number) { p.setR(RS[Math.min(b, 3)]); },
      enter() { p.reset(START); },
    });
  }

  // ── F3: Maze, Life and Replicator again (slide C4), now as Life-like network automata at resolution 9, each with its
  // own button (builds 1–3), on a small-world ring; builds 4 and 5 move all three to a small-world grid and then a
  // scale-free network (so do the buttons at the top), and what ran runs on. All three start from the same random
  // state of their network; a reset draws a new one. The networks are built when first shown. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f3')) {
    const SPECS: NetSpec[] = [{ kind: 'ws', n: 60, k: 8, p: 0.1 }, { kind: 'lat', side: 10, degree: 8, p: 0.1 }, { kind: 'npa', n: 100, m: 4, alpha: 1 }];
    const LAYOUT: Layout[] = ['ring', 'grid', 'force'], SIZE = 540, RHO = 0.4;
    const pics: Picture[] = [], firsts: Uint8Array[] = [];
    const pic = (k: number) => (pics[k] ??= picture(buildNet(SPECS[k], 31 + k, LAYOUT[k]), SIZE, 26, k ? 8 : 9, k === 2));
    const first = (k: number) => (firsts[k] ??= randomState(pic(k).net.n, RHO, makeRng(1984 + k)));
    let kind = 0;
    const btns = s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]'), nets = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-net]'));
    const panels = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas[data-rule]')).map((c, j) => {
      const [B, S] = c.dataset.rule!.split(',').map(Number), rule: Rule = { r: 9, B, S };
      let a = first(0).slice(), b = new Uint8Array(a.length);
      const paint = () => { const st = stage(c); if (st) drawNet(st, pic(kind), a); };
      const run = ticker(c, 5, () => { step(a, pic(kind).net, rule, b); [a, b] = [b, a]; }, paint);
      const restart = () => { a = first(kind).slice(); b = new Uint8Array(a.length); paint(); };
      sim(btns[j], 1 + j, {
        reset() { run.play(false); restart(); },
        play() { run.play(true); },
        reroll() { firsts[kind] = randomState(pic(kind).net.n, RHO, fresh()); },
      });
      onSize(c, paint);
      return { run, restart };
    });
    const show = (k: number) => {
      nets.forEach((x, i) => x.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(i === k)));
      if (k === kind) return;
      kind = k;
      for (const p of panels) p.restart();
    };
    nets.forEach((x, i) => x.addEventListener('click', () => show(i)));
    slide(s, {
      // a network picked by hand stays until a build picks one: 4, 5, or any step back or jump
      set(b: number, how: How) { if (b >= 4 || how !== 'next') show(b >= 5 ? 2 : b >= 4 ? 1 : 0); },
      enter() { firsts.length = 0; kind = -1; show(0); },
      leave() { for (const p of panels) p.run.play(false); },
    });
  }
</script>
